0 (perhaps on the other side of the world. It also provides a means of building larger scale collections of)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2281 0 313 341 sb
0 (documents that act as journals, books or encyclopedias. The format is also intended to act as a building block)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2460 0 313 408 sb
0 (for creating wide area groupware applications.)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 1048 0 313 475 sb
0 (HTML+ follows on from an earlier standard - HTML, see [Berners-Lee 93a], which has been widely used as)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2447 0 313 575 sb
0 (the basis for hypertext documents in the World Wide Web. The new format grew out of experience with)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2348 0 313 642 sb
0 (HTML, culminating in the desire to add new features, e.g. inline images, tables, and form fields for greater)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2409 0 313 709 sb
0 (flexibility in querying remote information sources. This document specifies the HTML+ format and suggests)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2459 0 313 776 sb
0 (ways in which browsers can choose to render it on a variety of different display types.)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 1939 0 313 843 sb
[89 0 0 -89 0 0] /Helvetica-Bold/Helvetica-BoldWE sf
0 (2.)false 0 83 0 0 0 0 74 0 313 1014 sb
0 (HTML+ and SGML)false 0 83 0 0 0 0 761 0 513 1014 sb
[56 0 0 -56 0 0] /Times-Roman/Times-RomanWE sf
0 (HTML+ itself is based on the Standardised General Markup Language \050SGML\051, an international standard for)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2462 0 313 1154 sb
0 (document markup that is becoming increasingly important. The term markup derives from the way proof-)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2384 0 313 1221 sb
0 (readers have traditionally pencilled in marks that indicate how the document should be revised.)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2148 0 313 1288 sb
0 (SGML grew out of a decade of work addressing the need for capturing the logical elements of documents as)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2438 0 313 1388 sb
0 (opposed to the processing functions to be performed on those elements. SGML is essentially an extensible)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2397 0 313 1455 sb
0 (document description language, based on a notation for embedding tags into the body of a document's text. It)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2458 0 313 1522 sb
0 (is defined by the international standard ISO 8879. The markup structure permitted for each class of documents)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2493 0 313 1589 sb
0 (is defined by an SGML Data Type Definition, usually abbreviated to DTD.)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 1690 0 313 1656 sb
0 (Working groups in ISO have recently produced a range of SGML DTDs for documents, e.g. ISO 12083 defines)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2508 0 313 1756 sb
0 (DTDs for books and ISO 10744, which defines the HyTime standard for hypermedia/time-based documents.)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2444 0 313 1823 sb
0 (These standards are large and complex, and perhaps best suited as interchange standards that facilitate)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2303 0 313 1890 sb
0 (conversion between proprietary document formats. By contrast, HTML+ provides a lightweight delivery)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2357 0 313 1957 sb
0 (format that can be rendered by relatively simple browsers, and which has grown out of two years practical)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2393 0 313 2024 sb
0 (experience with wide-area hypertext information systems in the Internet community.)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 1911 0 313 2091 sb
[67 0 0 -67 0 0] /Helvetica-Bold/Helvetica-BoldWE sf
0 (HTML+ and HyTime)false 0 63 0 0 0 0 624 0 313 2187 sb
[56 0 0 -56 0 0] /Times-Roman/Times-RomanWE sf
0 (The HyTime standard provides a rich range of architectural forms, but is not aimed at run-time efficiency.)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2393 0 313 2302 sb
0 (Suggestions have been made as to how the HTML DTD could be adapted to comply with HyTime's clink)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2370 0 313 2369 sb
0 (architectural form [Kimber 93]. This would necessitate documents declaring links as external entities and the)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2461 0 313 2436 sb
0 (use of local names in link definitions, but in the absence of any immediate benefit, there has been little)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2316 0 313 2503 sb
0 (enthusiasm for this within the World Wide Web community. Instead, it is believed that a straightforward filter)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2489 0 313 2570 sb
0 (program should be used to map HTML and HTML+ documents into a format which is strictly compliant with)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2472 0 313 2637 sb
0 (HyTime, when this becomes appropriate.)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 928 0 313 2704 sb
[67 0 0 -67 0 0] /Helvetica-Bold/Helvetica-BoldWE sf
0 (A simple example of HTML+)false 0 63 0 0 0 0 893 0 313 2800 sb
[56 0 0 -56 0 0] /Times-Roman/Times-RomanWE sf
0 (The following is a simple example of an HTML+ document,which illustrates the basic ideas involved in)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2347 0 313 2915 sb
0 (SGML.)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 169 0 313 2982 sb
[56 0 0 -56 0 0] /Courier/CourierWE sf
0 (<title>A Simple HTML+ Document</title>)false 0 45 0 0 0 0 1292 0 413 3078 sb
0 (<h1 id="a1">This is a level one header</h1>)false 0 45 0 0 0 0 1462 0 413 3167 sb
0 (<p> This is some normal text which will wrap at the window margin. You)false 0 45 0 0 0 0 2380 0 413 3256 sb
0 (can emphasise <em>parts of the text</em> if you wish.)false 0 45 0 0 0 0 1802 0 413 3312 sb
0 (<p> This is a new paragraph. Notice that unlike title and header tags,)false 0 45 0 0 0 0 2380 0 413 3401 sb
0 (there is no matching end tag.)false 0 45 0 0 0 0 986 0 413 3457 sb
[56 0 0 -56 0 0] /Times-Roman/Times-RomanWE sf
0 (The text of the document includes tags which are enclosed in <angle brackets>. Many tags have matching end)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2481 0 313 3550 sb
0 (tags for which the tag name is preceded by the "/" character. The tags are used to markup the document's)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2358 0 313 3617 sb
0 (logical elements, for example, the title, headers and paragraphs. Tags may also be accompanied by parameters,)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2501 0 313 3684 sb
0 (e.g. the "id" attribute in the header tag, which is used to define potential destinations for hypertext jumps.)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2375 0 313 3751 sb
0 (Unlike most document formats, SGML leaves out the processing instructions that determine the precise)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2336 0 313 3851 sb
0 (appearence of the document, for example the font name and point size, the margins, tab settings and how much)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2504 0 313 3918 sb
0 (white space to leave before and after different elements. The rendering software makes these choices for itself)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2482 0 313 3985 sb
0 (\050perhaps guided by user preferences\051, and so can avoid problems with different page sizes or missing fonts.)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 2419 0 313 4052 sb
0 (2)false 0 50 0 0 0 0 28 0 1553 4321 sb
